# 01. Purpose

This document defines the security architecture for AEGIS.

It establishes the rules governing:

```text
identity
authentication
authorization
roles
permissions
sessions
privileged actions
human approval
audit logging
data access
service authentication
security events
incident response
accountability
```

The purpose is to ensure that AEGIS remains:

```text
secure
traceable
accountable
least-privileged
resilient
auditable
human-governed
```

throughout its lifecycle.

---

# 02. Security Objective

The primary security objective is:

> **Ensure that the right person or service can perform the right action on the right resource at the right time, under the right authority, with an immutable record of what happened.**

AEGIS must assume that:

```text
users can make mistakes
credentials can be compromised
services can fail
data can become stale
permissions can change
systems can be attacked
network connectivity can degrade
```

Security therefore cannot depend on a single control.

---

# 03. Security Principles

AEGIS follows these principles.

## 03.1 Zero Trust

Never assume that:

```text
user = trusted
device = trusted
network = trusted
service = trusted
```

Every sensitive request must be appropriately authenticated and authorized.

---

## 03.2 Least Privilege

Users and services receive only the permissions necessary for their responsibilities.

---

## 03.3 Explicit Authorization

Authentication answers:

> Who are you?

Authorization answers:

> What are you allowed to do?

These are separate concerns.

---

## 03.4 Human Accountability

Consequential operational actions must have an identifiable human actor or explicitly authorized service actor.

---

## 03.5 Defense in Depth

Security must exist across:

```text
browser
frontend
API
service layer
database
infrastructure
identity provider
logging
monitoring
```

---

## 03.6 Secure by Default

If a permission is not explicitly granted:

```text
DENY
```

---

## 03.7 Fail Safely

When security state becomes uncertain:

```text
do not guess
do not silently authorize
do not execute consequential actions
```

